{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BxTbDYeitGneWmehKNB8Pf","type":"tool_result","content":"115:CROSS_GROUP_PROMPT = \"\"\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to properly-named characters.\n116-\n117-DESCRIPTIVE HANDLES:\n118-{epithets}\n119-\n120-NAMED CHARACTERS:\n121-{proper_names}\n122-\n123-IMPORTANT: Read the context snippets carefully. If an epithet and proper name appear in contexts showing them as SEPARATE ENTITIES (confronting, fighting, observing each other), DO NOT link them.\n124-\n125-For each epithet that CLEARLY refers to a named character, return a match.\n126-\n127-Return JSON array:\n128-```json\n129-[\n130-  {{\"epithet\": \"the prince\", \"proper_name\": \"Prince Prospero\"}},\n131-  {{\"epithet\": \"my father\", \"proper_name\": \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"}}\n132-]\n133-```\n134-\n135-Return ONLY the JSON array. Include only matches you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\"\"\"\n136-\n137-PAIRWISE_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether two character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\n138-\n139-CRITICAL:\n140-- Use ONLY the evidence provided below (chapter appearances and context snippets).\n141-- Be conservative: false negatives (missing an alias) are better than false positives (merging different people).\n142-- Titles (Mr/Mrs/Miss/Dr/etc.) are not part of the name; treat them as address forms.\n143-\n144-Return ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n145-\n146-PAIRWISE_ALIAS_PROMPT = \"\"\"Decide whether these two names refer to the SAME PERSON in this novel.\n147-\n148-NAME A: {name_a}\n149-MENTIONS A: {mentions_a}\n150-CHAPTERS A: {chapters_a}\n151-CONTEXT A:\n152-{contexts_a}\n153-\n154-NAME B: {name_b}\n155-MENTIONS B: {mentions_b}\n156-CHAPTERS B: {chapters_b}\n157-CONTEXT B:\n158-{contexts_b}\n159-\n160-Rules:\n161-- Only say same_person=true if there is strong evidence they are the same character (name overlap, title variants, obvious nickname/full-name relationship, or clear contextual cues).\n162-- If the only overlap is a last name, be VERY cautious (family members/spouses share last names).\n163-- If they never appear in overlapping chapters, be cautious unless it's clearly a full-name/short-name relationship.\n164-\n165-Return JSON:\n166-{{\n167-  \"same_person\": true/false,\n168-  \"confidence\": 0.0-1.0,\n169-  \"canonical\": \"Which of the two names should be canonical if same_person=true (must be exactly one of the provided names)\",\n170-  \"alias\": \"The other name if same_person=true (must be exactly one of the provided names)\",\n171-  \"reason\": \"brief justification\"\n172-}}\"\"\"\n173-\n174-\n175-ALIAS_RESOLUTION_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying character aliases.\n176-\n177-Your task is to determine which character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\n178-\n179-IMPORTANT: Titles, ranks, and honorifics should be IGNORED when matching names.\n180-The underlying PERSON is what matters, not how they're addressed. Examples:\n181-- Military: \"SSgt Otto\" = \"Staff Sergeant Mark Otto\" = \"Mark Otto\" (but NOT just \"Otto\" alone)\n182-- Civilian: \"Mr. Smith\" = \"John Smith\" (but NOT just \"Smith\" alone - could be family member)\n183-- Religious: \"Father O'Brien\" = \"Patrick O'Brien\" (but NOT just \"O'Brien\" alone)\n184-- Academic: \"Professor Williams\" = \"Dr. Williams\" = \"Jane Williams\"\n185-\n186-CRITICAL RULES - BE CONSERVATIVE:\n187-1. Only merge names if they CLEARLY refer to the same individual with HIGH CONFIDENCE\n188-2. Different people with similar names should NEVER be merged\n189-3. Pay close attention to chapter appearances and context clues\n190-4. When in doubt, keep names SEPARATE - false negatives are better than false positives\n191-5. A bare FIRST name can merge with a full name (e.g., \"Mike\" → \"Michael Anderson\")\n192-6. A bare LAST name is RISKY - only merge if you're absolutely certain (could be family member)\n193-\n194-For the canonical name, prefer the FULL CIVILIAN NAME (first + last) over titled versions.\n195-\n196-NEVER merge:\n197-- Different characters who happen to share only a first name (e.g., two characters named Michael)\n198-- Different characters who share ONLY a last name (family members, spouses, siblings)\n199-  - \"Mr. Anderson\" (father) ≠ \"Michael\" (son) even if both are Andersons\n200-  - \"Robert Wilson\" ≠ \"Mrs. Wilson\" (his wife Emma)\n201-- Characters who appear in completely different parts of the book with no overlap\n202-- Names that are clearly different major characters (check mention counts - if both have 50+ mentions, they're probably separate people!)\n203-\n204-BE EXTREMELY CAUTIOUS WITH:\n205-- Bare last names (could be family members)\n206-- Gendered titles (Mr. vs Mrs. vs Miss suggests different people)\n207-- Characters with similar but different names (e.g., \"Michael\" vs \"Michelle\")\n208-\n209-SPECIFIC ANTI-PATTERNS (DO NOT MERGE):\n210-- Different first names + same last name (likely family members): spouses, siblings, parents/children\n211-- Single-word names with no overlap: completely different characters with unrelated names\n212-- Characters who co-occur but have different identities: appearing together ≠ being the same person\n213-\n214-ONLY merge when confident:\n215-1. Full name + partial name: full name contains the partial name as a component"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd8b482e-9533-45db-8854-fa9612acdb55","uuid":"959d5849-0aa5-43ce-9bdf-5d8c8c5b21c5","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"115:CROSS_GROUP_PROMPT = \"\"\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to properly-named characters.\n116-\n117-DESCRIPTIVE HANDLES:\n118-{epithets}\n119-\n120-NAMED CHARACTERS:\n121-{proper_names}\n122-\n123-IMPORTANT: Read the context snippets carefully. If an epithet and proper name appear in contexts showing them as SEPARATE ENTITIES (confronting, fighting, observing each other), DO NOT link them.\n124-\n125-For e