{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XKhhV1FDBcmHcT8oRTUZ9W","type":"tool_result","content":"   100→   - Problem: \"started_at\" and \"ended_at\" rows in timing may show formatting issues\n   101→   - Location: HTML template\n   102→\n   103→## Fix History\n   104→\n   105→### Attempt 1 Fixes Applied\n   106→1. **Cross-group epithet resolution** (consensus.py) - Did not produce expected results\n   107→2. **Article filtering for pronunciation** (cmu_proposer.py) - Partially worked\n   108→\n   109→### Attempt 2 Fixes Applied\n   110→1. **Proper name with article classification** (consensus.py:_is_descriptive_handle())\n   111→   - Partially worked: Profile now generated, mention count improved 3→6\n   112→   - Caused regression: \"the mummer\" incorrectly merged with Prince Prospero\n   113→\n   114→### Attempt 3 Fixes Applied\n   115→1. **Enhanced cross-group resolution with conflict detection** (consensus.py)\n   116→   - Added CRITICAL RULE #5 about conflict/opposition/confrontation\n   117→   - Increased epithet context from 3x100 to 4x150 chars\n   118→   - Added context snippets for proper names (3x120 chars)\n   119→   - **Result: DID NOT WORK** - merge still happening\n   120→\n   121→## Key Insight for Fix Phase\n   122→\n   123→**The prompt-based approach is not working.** Three attempts have tried to fix this via LLM prompt improvements:\n   124→1. Attempt 1: Cross-group epithet resolution\n   125→2. Attempt 2: Proper name with article classification (caused the regression)\n   126→3. Attempt 3: Enhanced conflict detection prompts (did not work)\n   127→\n   128→**New approach needed:** Instead of relying on LLM judgment, implement a **structural/heuristic check**:\n   129→\n   130→1. **Pre-filter approach:** Before sending epithet-to-proper-name pairs to LLM, check if the epithet appears in sentences that describe CONFRONTATION with the proper name:\n   131→   - Look for verbs like: \"pursued\", \"confronted\", \"seized\", \"approached\", \"retreating from\"\n   132→   - If the epithet is the OBJECT of such verbs where the proper name is the SUBJECT, they are likely different entities\n   133→\n   134→2. **Example for this text:**\n   135→   - \"Prince Prospero... rushed hurriedly through the six chambers\" + \"the retreating figure\"\n   136→   - \"seizing the mummer\" (revellers seize the mummer)\n   137→   - \"Prince Prospero... had approached... to within three or four feet of the retreating figure\"\n   138→   - These patterns show the mummer/figure is being ACTED UPON separately from Prospero\n   139→\n   140→3. **Implementation location:** `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` in `_candidate_pairs_for_merge()` or early in `_llm_cross_group_resolution()`\n   141→\n   142→4. **Pseudo-code:**\n   143→   ```python\n   144→   def _entities_in_confrontation(entity1: str, entity2: str, text: str) -> bool:\n   145→       \"\"\"Check if two entities appear in confrontational relationship.\"\"\"\n   146→       confrontation_verbs = ['pursued', 'confronted', 'seized', 'attacked',\n   147→                             'approached', 'retreating', 'chased', 'fled']\n   148→       # Find sentences containing both entities\n   149→       # Check if one entity is agent and other is patient of confrontation verb\n   150→       # Return True if they appear to be opposing entities\n   151→   ```\n   152→\n   153→## Output Files\n   154→- HTML: output/masque_of_red_death/report.html\n   155→- JSON: output/masque_of_red_death/analysis.json\n   156→\n   157→## Pipeline Notes (Attempt 4)\n   158→- Analysis completed successfully in ~7m\n   159→- 1 character detected: \"the Prince Prospero\" (alias: \"the mummer\")\n   160→- 1 character profile generated\n   161→- 73 pronunciation flags\n   162→- Character extraction time: 4m 28s (62.5% of total time)\n   163→- Note: LLM identity detection failed (server error 500)\n   164→- Note: Moral valence classification failed (server error 500)\